<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparison of state-of-the-art token compression strategies under different token retention ratios on video question-answering benchmarks.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Two-step Spatio-Temporal Token Compression</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Equipped with the SCC strategy, we further propose a two-step token compression strategy across both spatial and temporal dimensions.
Given a video <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}=\{\mathbf{v}_{1},...,\mathbf{v}_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}=\{\mathbf{v}_{1},...,\mathbf{v}_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> frames, the visual video tokens <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}=\{\mathbf{t}_{1},...,\mathbf{t}_{n}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}=\{\mathbf{t}_{1},...,\mathbf{t}_{n}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> can be derived by processing it through the visual encoder and the visual projector, where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of tokens per frame and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the hidden state size.
We first determine the representative tokens for each frame in the spatial dimension. For each frame <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{i}\in\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐓</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{i}\in\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> independent semantic regions, we use <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the representative tokens obtained through the SCC strategy.</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\prime}_{i}=\text{SCC}(\mathbf{t}_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{m^{\prime}_{i}\times d},i=1,...,n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mtext>SCC</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\prime}_{i}=\text{SCC}(\mathbf{t}_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{m^{\prime}_{i}\times d},i=1,...,n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">After spatial compression, we obtain the representative tokens for every frame in the video. Subsequently, we concatenate all <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> together to further consider
the redundancy temporally.</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}^{\prime}=\text{concat}[\mathbf{t}^{\prime}_{1},...,\mathbf{t}^{\prime}_{n}]\in\mathbb{R}^{M^{\prime}\times d};M^{\prime}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}m^{\prime}_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>concat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}^{\prime}=\text{concat}[\mathbf{t}^{\prime}_{1},...,\mathbf{t}^{\prime}_{n}]\in\mathbb{R}^{M^{\prime}\times d};M^{\prime}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}m^{\prime}_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, all representative tokens from each frame of the video are included. However, distinct semantic regions within the spatial dimension of each frame may still share overlaps across frames. That is semantic regions appearing in one frame may also appear in other frames. Therefore, to avoid redundancy as much as possible, similar to spatial fusion, we apply the SCC strategy again temporally to further compress similar representative tokens across the sequence:
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T^{r}}=\text{SCC}(\mathbf{T^{\prime}})\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐫</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>SCC</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T^{r}}=\text{SCC}(\mathbf{T^{\prime}})\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d}.</annotation></semantics></math>
The representative tokens <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T^{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐫</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T^{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> comprehensively cover all spatio-temporally distinct semantic regions without redundancy or overlap. Treating them as retained tokens, we select the most suitable semantic regions for all tokens inspired by ToMe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we consider all tokens <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T^{a}}=\text{concat}[\mathbf{t}_{1},...,\mathbf{t}_{n}]\in\mathbb{R}^{(n\cdot m)\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>concat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T^{a}}=\text{concat}[\mathbf{t}_{1},...,\mathbf{t}_{n}]\in\mathbb{R}^{(n\cdot m)\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> as the source tokens, and the retained tokens <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T^{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐫</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T^{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the target tokens. As shown in Eqn. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.2 Two-step Spatio-Temporal Token Compression ‣ 3 LLaVA-Scissor ‣ LLaVA-Scissor: Token Compression with Semantic Connected Components for Video LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), we first compute the similarity map and find the most similar token <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{r}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>𝐫</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{r}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T^{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐫</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T^{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> for each token <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{a}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{a}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T^{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T^{a}}</annotation></semantics></math>, storing the indices in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E7X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{S}=\frac{\mathbf{T^{a}}\cdot(\mathbf{T^{r}})^{\top}}{||\mathbf{T^{a}}||\cdot||\mathbf{T^{r}}||}\in\mathbb{R}^{(n\cdot m)\times M};I=\text{argmax}_{i\in M}\mathcal{S}(i)\in\mathbb{R}^{(n\cdot m)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐫</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐫</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>argmax</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{S}=\frac{\mathbf{T^{a}}\cdot(\mathbf{T^{r}})^{\top}}{||\mathbf{T^{a}}||\cdot||\mathbf{T^{r}}||}\in\mathbb{R}^{(n\cdot m)\times M};I=\text{argmax}_{i\in M}\mathcal{S}(i)\in\mathbb{R}^{(n\cdot m)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector that stores the index of the most similar target token in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T^{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐫</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T^{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> for each token in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T^{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T^{a}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}=j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}=j</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{a}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{a}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is closest to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{r}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>𝐫</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{r}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, for each source token <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{a}}_{i}\in\mathbf{T^{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{a}}_{i}\in\mathbf{T^{a}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we assign it to the most similar target token <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{r}}_{j}\in\mathbf{T^{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>𝐫</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐫</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{r}}_{j}\in\mathbf{T^{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> according to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and perform an average merge to obtain the final compressed token <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{fin}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>𝐟𝐢𝐧</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{fin}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{fin}}_{j}=\frac{\sum^{n\cdot m}_{i=1}\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{a}}_{i}\mathbbm{1}(I_{i}=j)+\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{r}}_{j}}{\sum^{n\cdot m}_{i=1}\mathbbm{1}(I_{i}=j)+1},j=1,...,M." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐭</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐟𝐢𝐧</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>𝐫</mi></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{fin}}_{j}=\frac{\sum^{n\cdot m}_{i=1}\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{a}}_{i}\mathbbm{1}(I_{i}=j)+\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{r}}_{j}}{\sum^{n\cdot m}_{i=1}\mathbbm{1}(I_{i}=j)+1},j=1,...,M.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">We obtain the final merged tokens <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}^{\mathbf{fin}}=\text{concat}[\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{fin}}_{1},...,\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{fin}}_{M}]\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐟𝐢𝐧</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>concat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝐟𝐢𝐧</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>𝐟𝐢𝐧</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}^{\mathbf{fin}}=\text{concat}[\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{fin}}_{1},...,\mathbf{t}^{\mathbf{fin}}_{M}]\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> to represent the entire video.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experiment Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implement details.</span>
Similar to previous approaches, we choose LLaVA-OneVision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> as the base model architecture, which originally utilizes the CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> as the visual encoder and Qwen 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>]</cite> as the LLM. However, considering the rapid advancements in MLLM, its architecture and performance are no longer the most cutting-edge. For instance, SIGLIP (so400m-patch14-384) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">96</a>]</cite> is regarded as a superior vision encoder in many methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib115" title="" class="ltx_ref">115</a>]</cite>. Therefore, we replace the vision encoder in the original model with SIGLIP, employ the more advanced Qwen 2.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite> as the LLM, and retrain an enhanced version of the LLaVA-OneVision model using open-sourced Oryx <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Equipped with the enhanced LLaVA-OneVision model, we implement the proposed method and compare it with other approaches based on this model for a fair comparison. We primarily control the retention ratio through the similarity threshold <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. Unless otherwise specified, we set <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> to 0.05. All evaluation experiments in this paper are conducted on a single NVIDIA A100 GPU.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks and compared approaches.</span>
We validate the effectiveness of LLaVA-Scissor on a variety of benchmarks, including Video Question-Answering Benchmarks (ActivityNet-QA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>]</cite>, Video-ChatGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, Next-QA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>), Long Video Benchmarks (Egoschema <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, MLVU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib112" title="" class="ltx_ref">112</a>]</cite>, Video-MME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, VideoMMMU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>), and the comprehensive multi-choice benchmark MVBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. A detailed introduction to each dataset is provided in Sec. <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Benchmarks ‣ LLaVA-Scissor: Token Compression with Semantic Connected Components for Video LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> of appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We compare against several open-source representative methods: the image-based compression strategy VisionZip <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">86</a>]</cite>, the pooling-based PLLaVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite>, the pre-filling compression method FastV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and DyCoke <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite>, which applies compression in both the temporal and decoding stages. Notably, following the original paper settings, the ratio of dominant to contextual tokens in VisionZip is set to 5.4; the attention computation layer for both FastV and DyCoke is set to layer 3.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T2.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T2.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.4pt;padding-right:5.4pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Method</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.4pt;padding-right:5.4pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Retention ratio</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.4pt;padding-right:5.4pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">EgoSchema</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.4pt;padding-right:5.4pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">MLVU</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.4pt;padding-right:5.4pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">VideoMME</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.4pt;padding-right:5.4pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">VideoMMMU</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.4pt;padding-right:5.4pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Avg.(%)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.4pt;padding-right:5.4pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">LLaVA-OV-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite></span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.4pt;padding-right:5.4pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">100%</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.4pt;padding-right:5.4pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">58.08</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.4pt;padding-right:5.4pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">62.48</span></td>
